1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,705
Days before Anthropic's record IPO,

2
00:00:02,937 --> 00:00:06,002
AI's best-known skeptic made the case against it.

3
00:00:06,571 --> 00:00:08,858
Gary Marcus said the company might never turn

4
00:00:08,893 --> 00:00:09,543
a profit.

5
00:00:09,973 --> 00:00:11,378
Then, on the same show,

6
00:00:11,575 --> 00:00:14,257
Box CEO Aaron Levie made the case for

7
00:00:14,303 --> 00:00:14,559
it.

8
00:00:14,988 --> 00:00:16,266
Then the market voted.

9
00:00:16,695 --> 00:00:19,458
This is two hours of Big Technology in

10
00:00:19,505 --> 00:00:20,271
eight minutes:

11
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:21,838
the bear case,

12
00:00:21,873 --> 00:00:22,651
the bull case,

13
00:00:22,686 --> 00:00:23,951
what actually happened,

14
00:00:24,149 --> 00:00:26,146
and the one number both of them agree

15
00:00:26,192 --> 00:00:27,307
will decide it.

16
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:28,226
Part one.

17
00:00:28,656 --> 00:00:29,619
The bear case.

18
00:00:30,049 --> 00:00:31,268
First, a surprise.

19
00:00:31,756 --> 00:00:34,913
Gary Marcus, the scientist who spent years saying

20
00:00:34,995 --> 00:00:36,945
chatbots alone would hit a wall,

21
00:00:37,212 --> 00:00:39,244
actually praises Anthropic.

22
00:00:39,732 --> 00:00:41,276
He says its coding tool,

23
00:00:41,543 --> 00:00:45,327
Claude Code, mixes language models with classic rule-based

24
00:00:45,351 --> 00:00:46,140
software,

25
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,112
which is exactly what he argued AI needed.

26
00:00:49,472 --> 00:00:51,423
So the product isn't his problem.

27
00:00:51,783 --> 00:00:52,688
The price is.

28
00:00:53,257 --> 00:00:56,067
Going in, the IPO was expected to value

29
00:00:56,113 --> 00:00:58,865
Anthropic at up to $1.4 trillion.

30
00:00:59,642 --> 00:01:01,685
Marcus wrote that the company has never made

31
00:01:01,709 --> 00:01:02,359
a profit,

32
00:01:02,719 --> 00:01:04,936
except for one quarter he calls anomalous and

33
00:01:05,006 --> 00:01:06,132
heavily subsidized.

34
00:01:06,492 --> 00:01:09,569
And it carries nearly $900 billion in future

35
00:01:09,603 --> 00:01:10,277
commitments.

36
00:01:10,706 --> 00:01:12,947
His analogy: you can make the world's best

37
00:01:13,028 --> 00:01:15,246
lemonade, but if you sell it for less

38
00:01:15,269 --> 00:01:16,534
than the lemons cost,

39
00:01:16,581 --> 00:01:17,858
it's still a bad business.

40
00:01:18,287 --> 00:01:19,878
His bottom line is blunt.

41
00:01:20,238 --> 00:01:22,513
He wouldn't be shocked to see Anthropic go

42
00:01:22,583 --> 00:01:23,512
out of business.

43
00:01:24,417 --> 00:01:26,286
His most likely outcome?

44
00:01:26,414 --> 00:01:27,552
It gets sold,

45
00:01:27,749 --> 00:01:29,920
for a lot less than a trillion dollars.

46
00:01:30,408 --> 00:01:32,695
If you want the AI money story explained

47
00:01:32,718 --> 00:01:33,717
like this every week,

48
00:01:33,798 --> 00:01:36,317
subscribe now, so you don't miss the next

49
00:01:36,387 --> 00:01:36,700
one.

50
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:38,988
So why is he so sure the growth

51
00:01:39,022 --> 00:01:40,044
won't last?

52
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:40,971
Part two.

53
00:01:41,331 --> 00:01:42,991
The trillion-pound baby.

54
00:01:43,896 --> 00:01:45,417
Marcus has a favorite joke.

55
00:01:45,731 --> 00:01:48,308
A father tweets that his baby doubled its

56
00:01:48,355 --> 00:01:49,214
weight in a month,

57
00:01:49,574 --> 00:01:51,280
and projects that by college,

58
00:01:51,304 --> 00:01:53,440
the kid will weigh a trillion pounds.

59
00:01:54,345 --> 00:01:55,228
Growth like that,

60
00:01:55,274 --> 00:01:57,770
Marcus says, always tails off.

61
00:01:58,130 --> 00:02:00,592
And he thinks Anthropic's already has.

62
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:02,124
In the second quarter,

63
00:02:02,379 --> 00:02:04,829
revenue grew 147%,

64
00:02:04,852 --> 00:02:07,511
during what he calls the token-maxing fad,

65
00:02:07,743 --> 00:02:09,914
when companies told staff to use as much

66
00:02:10,007 --> 00:02:11,412
AI as they could.

67
00:02:11,772 --> 00:02:12,979
In the third quarter,

68
00:02:13,176 --> 00:02:15,162
growth was 47%.

69
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:17,356
Then there's competition.

70
00:02:17,553 --> 00:02:20,293
Since 2023, he has argued that everyone is

71
00:02:20,363 --> 00:02:21,779
building the same thing,

72
00:02:22,139 --> 00:02:24,008
which means price wars.

73
00:02:24,438 --> 00:02:26,830
Cheaper Chinese models made that real this year,

74
00:02:26,865 --> 00:02:30,139
he says. That leaves Anthropic one niche:

75
00:02:30,498 --> 00:02:32,263
the very top-end models.

76
00:02:32,623 --> 00:02:35,316
Host Alex Kantrowitz put it this way:

77
00:02:35,885 --> 00:02:38,486
if an associate professor can do the job,

78
00:02:38,846 --> 00:02:40,495
why pay the full professor?

79
00:02:40,924 --> 00:02:42,863
And each step up costs more.

80
00:02:43,351 --> 00:02:46,091
One billion to impress users this year,

81
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:49,098
Marcus warns, ten billion next year,

82
00:02:49,365 --> 00:02:51,106
a hundred billion after that.

83
00:02:51,536 --> 00:02:53,370
Kantrowitz added more pressure:

84
00:02:53,858 --> 00:02:56,993
five customers make up 19% of revenue,

85
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:59,361
and some big ones are pulling back.

86
00:03:00,139 --> 00:03:04,075
Marcus's conclusion: Anthropic has to stay a rocket.

87
00:03:04,562 --> 00:03:07,534
Every customer who pulls back makes escape velocity

88
00:03:07,615 --> 00:03:09,566
harder. That's the bear case.

89
00:03:09,601 --> 00:03:11,911
Now meet the man who thinks it's wrong.

90
00:03:11,967 --> 00:03:12,815
Part three.

91
00:03:13,244 --> 00:03:14,150
The bull case.

92
00:03:14,637 --> 00:03:16,565
Aaron Levie runs Box,

93
00:03:16,762 --> 00:03:18,875
which sends huge amounts of business work through

94
00:03:18,921 --> 00:03:19,850
AI models.

95
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:21,812
He starts with a disclaimer:

96
00:03:22,172 --> 00:03:24,239
he's not giving financial advice,

97
00:03:24,471 --> 00:03:25,899
and he won't say what the shares are

98
00:03:25,934 --> 00:03:26,398
worth.

99
00:03:26,758 --> 00:03:28,221
But on the market itself,

100
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:29,440
he's very bullish.

101
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:30,775
His first number:

102
00:03:30,938 --> 00:03:34,630
Anthropic's revenue went from $1.4 billion

103
00:03:34,990 --> 00:03:36,638
in the third quarter of last year

104
00:03:36,998 --> 00:03:39,761
to $17 billion this third quarter.

105
00:03:40,539 --> 00:03:43,070
Twelve times. He doesn't expect that again,

106
00:03:43,152 --> 00:03:45,264
he says, but the direction holds.

107
00:03:45,624 --> 00:03:46,727
His second number:

108
00:03:46,994 --> 00:03:50,779
customers spending at least $100,000 a year

109
00:03:51,348 --> 00:03:55,644
grew from about 950 to about 11,900.

110
00:03:56,317 --> 00:03:57,327
Companies, he says,

111
00:03:57,350 --> 00:03:58,743
are voting with their wallets.

112
00:03:59,521 --> 00:04:00,949
Why will it keep going?

113
00:04:01,309 --> 00:04:03,388
Because coding was just the start.

114
00:04:04,061 --> 00:04:06,639
Once AI agents can use computers and write

115
00:04:06,662 --> 00:04:07,800
their own software,

116
00:04:08,159 --> 00:04:10,737
almost every job becomes code-adjacent:

117
00:04:11,515 --> 00:04:14,034
product design, cybersecurity,

118
00:04:14,266 --> 00:04:15,892
research, sales.

119
00:04:16,565 --> 00:04:18,550
For the first time in office work,

120
00:04:18,597 --> 00:04:22,382
Levie says, output isn't limited by how fast

121
00:04:22,452 --> 00:04:23,415
people can type.

122
00:04:23,984 --> 00:04:26,422
The only limit is how much computing power

123
00:04:26,469 --> 00:04:28,918
you buy. And his favorite chart,

124
00:04:29,081 --> 00:04:31,008
from Anthropic's own filing:

125
00:04:31,670 --> 00:04:33,586
in early 2025,

126
00:04:33,702 --> 00:04:36,581
every dollar spent on computing brought back 40

127
00:04:36,651 --> 00:04:38,276
cents. This year,

128
00:04:38,369 --> 00:04:39,983
a dollar seventy-two.

129
00:04:40,749 --> 00:04:41,852
People, he says,

130
00:04:42,049 --> 00:04:43,315
should update their views.

131
00:04:43,675 --> 00:04:45,451
So how does he answer Marcus?

132
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:46,355
Part four.

133
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:48,294
Round two.

134
00:04:48,653 --> 00:04:51,742
Kantrowitz put Marcus's biggest worries to Levie.

135
00:04:52,055 --> 00:04:52,903
The debt?

136
00:04:53,576 --> 00:04:56,130
Levie says everything depends on one thing:

137
00:04:56,397 --> 00:04:58,684
whether growth continues at some level.

138
00:04:59,044 --> 00:05:00,519
If the revenue comes,

139
00:05:00,623 --> 00:05:02,017
the commitments are fine.

140
00:05:02,376 --> 00:05:03,235
If it doesn't,

141
00:05:03,305 --> 00:05:04,443
they blow up the company.

142
00:05:05,348 --> 00:05:06,765
Token-maxing?

143
00:05:07,125 --> 00:05:09,342
He calls it a two-week fashion,

144
00:05:09,656 --> 00:05:12,965
not the reason Anthropic's revenue run rate passed

145
00:05:13,011 --> 00:05:14,091
$80 billion.

146
00:05:14,764 --> 00:05:16,030
Diminishing returns?

147
00:05:16,459 --> 00:05:19,060
He says improving a model for one field,

148
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:21,428
like cybersecurity or law,

149
00:05:21,626 --> 00:05:23,634
costs millions in training data,

150
00:05:23,773 --> 00:05:25,306
not hundreds of billions.

151
00:05:25,666 --> 00:05:27,988
And each of those fields is worth trillions

152
00:05:28,023 --> 00:05:29,068
of dollars of work.

153
00:05:29,845 --> 00:05:31,958
Cheap Chinese and open models?

154
00:05:32,864 --> 00:05:34,722
Levie sees a rising tide.

155
00:05:34,989 --> 00:05:37,601
Companies use a top model to plan and

156
00:05:37,647 --> 00:05:39,307
cheap models for the bulk work,

157
00:05:39,621 --> 00:05:41,177
so they spend more on both.

158
00:05:41,955 --> 00:05:43,719
Marcus answers with a warning:

159
00:05:44,207 --> 00:05:45,821
more than a dozen studies,

160
00:05:45,844 --> 00:05:48,653
he says, show companies struggling to get a

161
00:05:48,735 --> 00:05:49,455
return,

162
00:05:49,884 --> 00:05:52,578
and hallucinations haven't gone away.

163
00:05:53,356 --> 00:05:54,772
Marcus gave ground too.

164
00:05:55,132 --> 00:05:57,071
He called his doubt about raising the money

165
00:05:57,152 --> 00:05:58,430
his weakest argument,

166
00:05:58,789 --> 00:06:01,878
as reports said the IPO was oversubscribed.

167
00:06:02,365 --> 00:06:04,978
He didn't dispute the tenfold revenue growth.

168
00:06:05,407 --> 00:06:07,741
And he agreed that the fallout from Jensen

169
00:06:07,810 --> 00:06:08,728
Huang's scandal

170
00:06:09,157 --> 00:06:11,711
could help Anthropic against Chinese rivals.

171
00:06:12,199 --> 00:06:14,405
"The best argument you've made," he told the

172
00:06:14,451 --> 00:06:14,950
host.

173
00:06:15,624 --> 00:06:16,611
And on one point,

174
00:06:16,808 --> 00:06:18,306
Levie conceded outright.

175
00:06:18,793 --> 00:06:20,953
Could the bills come due before the industries

176
00:06:20,999 --> 00:06:21,615
change?

177
00:06:22,288 --> 00:06:25,121
"Absolutely possible," he said.

178
00:06:25,167 --> 00:06:26,804
Part five. Then the market voted.

179
00:06:27,373 --> 00:06:28,604
On November 19th,

180
00:06:28,650 --> 00:06:31,761
Anthropic listed at $83 a share.

181
00:06:32,121 --> 00:06:34,873
It opened around $111 and closed its first

182
00:06:34,908 --> 00:06:36,475
day near $97.

183
00:06:36,905 --> 00:06:39,900
By Friday, it had slipped to about $87,

184
00:06:39,923 --> 00:06:41,479
still above its listing price,

185
00:06:41,839 --> 00:06:43,441
as tech stocks fell around it.

186
00:06:43,871 --> 00:06:45,612
The deal raised more than $80 billion,

187
00:06:45,694 --> 00:06:48,689
and up to $93 billion if the underwriters

188
00:06:48,724 --> 00:06:49,943
take their extra shares.

189
00:06:50,430 --> 00:06:52,671
So far, neither man is proven right.

190
00:06:52,903 --> 00:06:53,670
On the show,

191
00:06:53,716 --> 00:06:56,154
both sides agreed the first day says little.

192
00:06:56,514 --> 00:06:58,232
The real test is time.

193
00:06:58,546 --> 00:07:00,508
Which brings us back to the one number

194
00:07:00,601 --> 00:07:02,505
both of them agree will decide it.

195
00:07:02,567 --> 00:07:04,726
Part six. The deciding number.

196
00:07:05,086 --> 00:07:07,014
Strip away the babies and the lemonade,

197
00:07:07,211 --> 00:07:09,057
and both men are making the same bet

198
00:07:09,103 --> 00:07:10,439
in opposite directions.

199
00:07:11,112 --> 00:07:14,003
Levie says everything depends on whether growth continues.

200
00:07:14,432 --> 00:07:16,987
Marcus says Anthropic has to stay a rocket.

201
00:07:17,346 --> 00:07:19,494
So the number to watch is next quarter's

202
00:07:19,541 --> 00:07:22,687
growth. If it bounces back from 47%,

203
00:07:22,710 --> 00:07:23,999
the bull case gets stronger.

204
00:07:24,568 --> 00:07:27,691
If it keeps sliding while those $900 billion

205
00:07:27,761 --> 00:07:29,026
in commitments come due,

206
00:07:29,386 --> 00:07:30,570
the bear case does.

207
00:07:31,058 --> 00:07:33,113
And as Ed Zitron argued in our last

208
00:07:33,183 --> 00:07:33,868
episode,

209
00:07:34,355 --> 00:07:37,653
anything under about $21 billion in revenue this

210
00:07:37,722 --> 00:07:39,301
quarter would be a problem.

211
00:07:39,870 --> 00:07:41,507
This is their analysis,

212
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:43,109
not financial advice.

213
00:07:43,597 --> 00:07:46,035
This was two hours of Big Technology in

214
00:07:46,082 --> 00:07:46,825
eight minutes.

215
00:07:47,185 --> 00:07:49,344
Both full episodes are linked below.

216
00:07:49,774 --> 00:07:51,654
And for the AI money story,

217
00:07:51,747 --> 00:07:53,187
explained every week,

218
00:07:53,315 --> 00:07:54,487
subscribe.

219
00:07:54,533 --> 00:07:56,019
And tell us in the comments:

220
